;; spec:   stdlib §4.15 (row future: "@f is (join f)"), §2.11, syntax §3.11, types §2.9
;; expect: accept
;; result: 42
;; audit:  clean
;; roots:  ../../lib
;; covers: future
;; The note of row future says @f is (join f), as Clojure's is. This case belongs to package L12, which
;; pinned the state of the checker as it was: no rule of @ for a Task (types/infer/solve.rs, solve_deref
;; knew a Cell, an Atom and a Weak), so it was a reject case that said `no implementation of Deref for
;; (Task i64)`. The owner's standing rule of 2026-10-01 (unless it breaks memory safety, Clojure's
;; ergonomics are the ones fibber replicates; `join` is already safe) amended types §2.9 and syntax
;; §3.11 with a fourth built-in instance, (Deref (Task a) a), whose deref is `join`. Both tools now
;; accept it, so the case is flipped to the accepted behaviour: @f of a future is the task's value,
;; and cases ownership/232 to 236 pin the behaviour of @ on a task in depth. Case 655 gets its @f check.
(ns main (:use fib.core fib.async))

(defun main () -> i64
  (let ((f (future 42)))
    @f))
